course: dictionary
lesson: 3034
title: Earlobes in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An earlobe is the soft, round part at the bottom of the outside of your ear. You can find yours with your fingers right now.
explain: Context helps you here. «Pierced earlobes» tells you holes are made in them for earrings, and «tug your earlobe» tells you it is something you can pull gently. Both clues point to the same soft part of the ear.
explain: The hens sentence says, «hens with white earlobes lay white eggs». Here the word is about a body part you can see and compare, and it is a useful clue in a science lesson.
fact: An earlobe is the soft, round part at the bottom of the outside of your ear.
fact: «Pierced earlobes» means earlobes with a hole made through them, so earrings can be worn.
fact: «Tug your earlobe» means pull it gently.
fact: In science, earlobes can be attached to the head or hang free.
fact: Earlobe can also be written as two words: ear lobe.
exercise: What is an earlobe? | The hard bone inside your head | The top of your head | *The soft, round part at the bottom of the outside of your ear | The tip of your nose
exercise: Mia says, «Pierced earlobes have holes for earrings.» What does pierced mean here? | Pulled very hard | *Has a hole made through it | Painted bright blue | Hidden under hair
exercise: «Tug your earlobe gently.» What should you do with your earlobe? | Turn it into a button | Hide it under a hat | Tap it on the table | *Pull it gently with your fingers
test: In «hens with white earlobes lay white eggs», what are earlobes? | *A feature you can see on the hen | A kind of egg | A type of food | A sound the hen makes
test: Why are earlobes mentioned in science lessons? | They tell the time | They help a hen lay eggs | *Some are attached to the head and some hang free | They make the best earrings
exam: Which is true? | An earlobe is a type of earring | An earlobe is a hard bone inside your ear | An earlobe is the top of your head | *An earlobe is the soft, round part at the bottom of the outside of your ear
